<article class="env-lemma" id="0DWL">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0DWL">Lemma <span data-tag="0DWL">49.10.3</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $B = k[x_1, \ldots , x_ n]/(f_1, \ldots , f_ n)$ be a global complete intersection over $k$ of dimension $0$. Then $\omega _{B/k}$ is invertible. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Noether normalization, see Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/00OY" data-tag="00OY">10.115.4</a> we see that there exists a finite injection $k \to B$, i.e., $\dim _ k(B) < \infty $. Hence $\omega _{B/k} = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ k(B, k)$ as a $B$-module. By Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0AX0" data-tag="0AX0">47.15.8</a> we see that $R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (B, k)$ is a dualizing complex for $B$ and by Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0A71" data-tag="0A71">47.13.3</a> we see that $R\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits (B, k)$ is equal to $\omega _{B/k}$ placed in degree $0$. Thus it suffices to show that $B$ is Gorenstein (Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0DW9" data-tag="0DW9">47.21.4</a>). This is true by Dualizing Complexes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0DWA" data-tag="0DWA">47.21.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>